  just a plumbing one. Where it's stored and how long it lives determine what an XSS bug or a shared machine can leak.
  I'll treat it as such rather than picking a default quietly.

  First question, and it's the one everything else hangs off.

  [1mThe fork:[0m who creates the userId — the server, as a consequence of authenticating, or the client, before anyone has
  proven who they are.

  - [1mServer issues it on successful login[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns it, the app stores it. Buys a real identity: the value means
    something because the server vouched for it. Costs a dependency on the API contract (right now [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub
    returning [38;5;153m{success, user}[39m at [38;5;153mapp.js:7[39m, so we'd be designing against an endpoint that doesn't exist yet). This is the
    hard-to-change one — it defines the client/server boundary.
  - [1mClient generates and persists it[0m — a UUID minted on first visit, reused forever. Buys immediate progress with no
    backend. Costs correctness as [3midentity[0m: it identifies a browser, not a person. Two people on one machine share it;
    one person on two devices looks like two users. It is spoofable, so it can never gate anything that matters.
  - [1mBoth, separate values[0m — a client device/visitor id for analytics plus a server-issued user id after auth. Buys
    honest semantics for each. Costs two things to thread through every future form instead of one.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m server-issued. You said it should identify the [3mactual[0m user, and a client-minted value cannot do
  that — it would quietly become a thing later code trusts for authorization. If the backend isn't ready, I'd still
  design the seam that way and stub the server's half, so the contract is right even while the value is fake.
